WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.940
How does Boyer–Moore use the

2
00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:03.540
good suffix? We will find

3
00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.400
the suffix already matched before

4
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.180
a mismatch, choose a safe

5
00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:08.900
pattern slide, and convert that

6
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.480
slide into the match-jump table

7
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:11.740
used by the course code.

8
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:15.160
Suppose right-to-left comparisons matched the

9
00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:16.660
last m minus k pattern

10
00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.540
characters before failing at k.

11
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.220
That matched text suffix is

12
00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:22.800
information we should preserve when

13
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.400
choosing the next possible alignment.

14
00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:26.640
First look for an earlier

15
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.260
full occurrence of that suffix

16
00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.100
in the pattern, preceded by

17
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.700
a different character from P

18
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:33.280
at k. Choose the rightmost

19
00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.940
suitable occurrence, which gives the

20
00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.100
smallest positive slide preserving this

21
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:38.820
useful match.

22
00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.020
Why insist on a different

23
00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.500
preceding character? If the earlier

24
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.120
suffix had the same preceding

25
00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.100
pattern character, aligning it would

26
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.780
reproduce the mismatch we already

27
00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.480
know, so it cannot supply

28
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:51.880
the next viable alignment.

29
00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:54.820
The slide example uses pattern

30
00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:56.540
R A T S A

31
00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:57.920
N D C A T

32
00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:00.360
S, length eleven, with mismatch

33
00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.440
at position eight after matching

34
00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.140
A T S. The earlier

35
00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:05.600
A T S ends at

36
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.900
position four and is preceded

37
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.240
by R rather than C.

38
00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:11.720
The actual pattern slide is

39
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.680
eleven minus four, which equals

40
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.820
seven. But j has already

41
00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:17.980
moved three places left, so

42
00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:19.380
its increment is those three

43
00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:21.740
places plus seven, giving match

44
00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:22.560
jump ten.

45
00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:24.960
If no suitable full suffix

46
00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.520
occurrence exists, try aligning the

47
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.220
longest suffix of that matched

48
00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.040
portion which is also a

49
00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.300
prefix of the pattern. This

50
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.040
second case preserves a partial

51
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.520
overlap that could still grow

52
00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:37.620
into a complete match.

53
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:39.840
If neither a full occurrence

54
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:41.860
nor a prefix overlap works,

55
00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:43.840
move the pattern beyond the

56
00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:45.980
matched suffix. In that third

57
00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.040
case the actual pattern slide

58
00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:49.960
is m, while the pointer

59
00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:51.840
increment still includes the m

60
00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.580
minus k matched positions.

61
00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.340
Now build the course table

62
00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:57.500
for W O W W

63
00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:59.520
O W, with positions listed

64
00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:01.580
in increasing k. When no

65
00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:02.940
suffix has matched at k

66
00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.700
equal to six, the convention

67
00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:06.160
is slide one and match

68
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:06.700
jump one.

69
00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:08.640
For k equal to five,

70
00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:10.620
suffix W can align to

71
00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:12.240
a suitable earlier W with

72
00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.280
slide two, giving pointer increment

73
00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.640
three. For k equal to

74
00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.580
four, suffix O W has

75
00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:20.380
an earlier full occurrence preceded

76
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:21.580
by the same W as

77
00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:23.400
the mismatch position, so that

78
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:24.560
occurrence is excluded.

79
00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.700
For that k equal to

80
00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.600
four case, a prefix overlap

81
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:30.760
W instead gives slide five,

82
00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:32.400
and the pointer increment is

83
00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.800
two plus five, or seven.

84
00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.580
For k equal to three,

85
00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:37.680
two and one, the useful

86
00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.200
shift is three, giving jumps

87
00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:41.260
six, seven and eight respectively.

88
00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:43.840
Reading entries from k one

89
00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:45.760
through six, the slide table

90
00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:48.900
is three, three, three, five,

91
00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:52.340
two, one. The corresponding match-jump

92
00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:54.920
table is eight, seven, six,

93
00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.780
seven, three, one, because each

94
00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:59.920
entry adds its matched-suffix length.

95
00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.340
The deck teaches these cases

96
00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:04.240
and tables without developing a

97
00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:07.700
complete preprocessing implementation. Do not

98
00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.920
invent its preprocessing cost or

99
00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.280
borrow a differently indexed good-suffix

100
00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:14.320
table; the number required by

101
00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:15.700
this scan is always the

102
00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:17.600
pointer increment, not just the

103
00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:17.920
slide.

104
00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:19.720
Things to take note of:

105
00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.520
identify the exact matched suffix,

106
00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:24.720
test full occurrence before prefix

107
00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.260
overlap, and check the preceding-character

108
00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:30.060
condition. Add the matched-suffix length

109
00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:31.400
when converting to the pointer

110
00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.640
increment, and label table order

111
00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:36.440
explicitly so an extracted PDF

112
00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:38.520
cannot silently reverse your answer,

113
00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:39.660
hmph.
